  "summary": "Three new preprints probe how non-Hermitian asymmetry and nonlinearity reshape edge-localized states beyond conventional bulk-boundary expectations.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how non-Hermitian asymmetry and nonlinearity alter the behavior of edge-localized states. Non-Hermitian edge accretion reports coupled skin-effect channels that amplify states bouncing off edges, departing from the usual OBC-within-PBC spectral picture. An experimental study of coupled nonlinear SSH chains shows that nonlinearity controls interchain exchange, enabling temporal routing of topological edge states. A third preprint extends the Ganeshan-Pixley-Das Sarma model to non-Hermitian quasiperiodic chains with asymmetric hopping and complex potentials, and attributes complex mobility edges to competing localization channels. What is new across these items is the combination of edge amplification, nonlinear control, and quasiperiodic localization in non-Hermitian settings. The shared focus is edge-state physics rather than a single unified result.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
